    "output_text": "```python\ndef max_subarray_sum(arr: list[int]) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Find the maximum subarray sum using Kadane's algorithm.\n    \n    Args:\n        arr: A list of integers (can include negative numbers).\n        \n    Returns:\n        The maximum sum of any contiguous subarray.\n        \n    Raises:\n        ValueError: If the input list is empty.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not arr:\n        raise ValueError(\"Input array must not be empty\")\n    \n    max_ending_here = arr[0]\n    max_so_far = arr[0]\n    \n    for i in range(1, len(arr)):\n        # Either extend the current subarray or start a new one at arr[i]\n        max_ending_here = max(arr[i], max_ending_here + arr[i])\n        # Update the global maximum if we found a better subarray\n        max_so_far = max(max_so_far, max_ending_here)\n    \n    return max_so_far\n```\n\n## Explanation of O(n) Time Complexity\n\nKadane's algorithm runs in **O(n)** time for the following reasons:\n\n1. **Single Pass**: The algorithm iterates through the input array exactly once, from index `1` to `n-1` (where `n` is the length of the array). This is a single linear scan.\n\n2. **Constant-Time Operations per Element**: For each element in the array, the algorithm performs a fixed number of operations:\n   - One addition (`max_ending_here + arr[i]`)\n   - One comparison via `max()` to decide whether to extend the current subarray or start a new one\n   - One comparison via `max()` to update the global maximum\n   \n   Each of these operations takes O(1) time.\n\n3. **No Nested Loops or Recursion**: Unlike brute-force approaches that check all O(n\u00b2) or O(n\u00b3) subarrays, Kadane's algorithm avoids nested loops entirely. It maintains only two variables (`max_ending_here` and `max_so_far`) that are updated in constant time at each step.\n\n4. **Dynamic Programming Insight**: The key insight is that the maximum subarray sum ending at position `i` depends only on the maximum subarray sum ending at position `i-1`. This optimal substructure allows us to build the solution incrementally in a single pass, rather than recomputing sums",
